      <header class="hero">
        <span class="hero__chapter">Tactic #1 · Stop Coding</span>
        <h1>Hello Agentic Coding</h1>
        <p class="hero__dek">The decisive move is not a better code-generation prompt. It is turning a prompt into a reliable engineering workflow—and turning the agent into a component you can call.</p>
        <ul class="hero__meta" aria-label="Chapter details">
          <li>Video spine · 21:51</li>
          <li>Lab · 45–75 min</li>
          <li>Course repo · tac/tac-1</li>
          <li>Level · weak beginner → operator</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to diagnose agentic work with the Core Four, explain the exact difference between AI coding and agentic coding, reproduce Dan’s interactive and headless demos, and teach why “replace yourself” is a leverage strategy rather than a surrender.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mobile-toc">
        <details>
          <summary>Chapter contents</summary>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#orientation">Orientation</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-role">The new role</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-mission">Mission and leverage</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-stop">Stop coding</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-core-four">The Core Four</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-ab">The A/B demo</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-tool">Why this tool class works</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-programmable">Programmable agent</a></li>
            <li><a href="#idea-paradox">Replace yourself</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lab">Graded lab</a></li>
