      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
        <span class="section-number">00</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Begin with the failure, not the slogan</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">You ask an AI to write a function. It gives you plausible code. You paste it into the repository, discover that it does not fit, repair two imports, run the tests, fix a test, create a branch, make the commit, and explain the result in Slack. The AI helped with one narrow slice. You still carried the workflow.</p>

        <p>That is the concrete failure TAC-1 addresses. The problem is not that the generated code was bad. The problem is that the unit of delegation was too small. You delegated <em>typing</em> while retaining every transition that makes typing useful: reading the repository, choosing a safe branch, creating files, running programs, observing output, committing a coherent change, and reporting what happened. AI coding accelerated an activity. Agentic coding delegates an outcome-producing chain.</p>

        <p>Dan’s lesson makes this distinction with almost comical economy. The source repository contains only seven files. Its README is exactly 35 lines. The generated programs print tiny messages. That smallness is deliberate. A realistic codebase would let you confuse complexity with the concept. Here the application code is so trivial that the only interesting variable is <strong>who moves the work through the engineering system</strong>.</p>

        <aside class="callout callout--tactic">
          <p class="callout__label">The chapter’s one-sentence thesis</p>
          <p>AI coding uses context, model, and prompt to generate code. Agentic coding keeps those three and adds tools that can be executed reliably in a long chain—so the prompt becomes a workflow and the agent performs engineering work.</p>
        </aside>

        <h3>What you are training</h3>
        <ul class="learning-objectives">
          <li><strong>Discrimination</strong>Tell code generation apart from workflow execution even when both begin with a prompt.</li>
          <li><strong>Diagnosis</strong>Locate failures in context, model, prompt, or tools instead of blindly rerolling.</li>
          <li><strong>Operation</strong>Reproduce the branch → write → run → commit → report cycle in the real TAC-1 repo.</li>
          <li><strong>Transfer</strong>Recognize how <code>claude -p</code> turns an interactive agent into a programmable building block.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How to use this chapter</h3>
        <p>Do not read it as a transcript recap. Each major idea repeats the same ten learning moves: failure, picture, one demo move, term, worked example, a small action, failure repair, current-practice overlay, retrieval, and teach-back. That repetition is intentional. Expertise is not the ability to recognize Dan’s phrases. It is the ability to retrieve the idea under pressure, operate it in the course repository, notice when it fails, and explain it without the chapter open.</p>

        <div class="callout callout--warning">
          <p class="callout__label">Prime directive for every exercise</p>
          <p>You type zero lines of application code by hand. “Stop coding” is a behavioral constraint, not a discussion topic. You may type prompts and terminal commands. When a generated file is imperfect, repair it through the agent so you practice controlling the loop.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-role">
        <span class="section-number">01</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">A new role: the Agentic Engineer</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The title matters only if the work changes. “Agentic Engineer” is not a badge for someone who uses AI often; it names an engineer who builds systems able to take engineering actions.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>The prompt expert who is still the workflow engine</h3>
          <p>Imagine an engineer who can produce excellent code with a one-shot prompt. They know how to attach a specification, point at the right files, and ask for tests. Yet after every answer they manually copy code, open files, run commands, move changes onto a branch, decide whether the output succeeded, and write a summary. Their prompts improved. Their role did not. The human remains the glue between every step.</p>
          <p>This is why “prompt engineer” is too narrow for Dan’s prediction. A clever request can improve a model’s answer. An agentic system changes who performs the sequence. If the human must relay every output into the next input, the human is still the orchestration layer. The system has intelligence but little agency.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the two-phase map</p>
          <h3>Move the boundary of delegation</h3>
          <div class="comparison" aria-label="AI coding and agentic coding roles compared">
            <article class="comparison__card">
              <div class="comparison__head"><h3>Phase 1 · AI coding</h3><p>The agent produces code; the human carries the workflow.</p></div>
              <div class="comparison__body">
                <div class="workflow workflow--stacked">
                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>context → prompt</code></div>
                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Agent</strong><code>generate code</code></div>
                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>write → run → fix → commit → report</code></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic">
              <div class="comparison__head"><h3>Phase 2 · Agentic coding</h3><p>The human specifies and reviews; the agent carries the workflow.</p></div>
              <div class="comparison__body">
                <div class="workflow workflow--stacked">
                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>goal + constraints + checks</code></div>
                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Agent</strong><code>branch → write → run → commit → report</code></div>
                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>review outcome + improve loop</code></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
          <p>The important movement is the boundary. The human does not disappear. The human moves from carrying transitions to designing them, setting constraints, and evaluating the result.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
          <h3>A 2023 bet becomes a visible phase change</h3>
          <p>Dan opens with a concrete personal bet: in 2023 he bought <em>agenticengineer.com</em>, predicting a role centered on building systems capable of taking engineering actions. The claim is not that engineers vanish. It is that an engineer’s leverage shifts from personally performing actions to constructing systems that can perform them.</p>
          <figure class="frame-card">
            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0115-phase-1-vs-phase-2.jpg" alt="Dan's phase diagram moving from AI coding in phase one to agentic coding in phase two" loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">1:55</span>Dan places AI coding and agentic coding on a two-phase timeline. Keep this scaffold visible: phase two is a superset, not a rejection, of phase one.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>His one move is conceptual before it is technical: name the era change. In phase one, prompt-to-code makes code abundant and gives rise to vibe coding. In phase two, the engineering response is to command systems that take actions. That frame prevents the later terminal demo from shrinking into “Claude Code can run Git.” Git commands are merely the visible actions; role redesign is the lesson.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
          <h3>Phase-two Agentic Engineer</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term">
            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p>
            <p><strong>Agentic Engineer:</strong> an engineer who builds systems that can take actions as engineers can.</p>
          </aside>
          <p>In plain English: you build the worker and the work path, not merely the artifact at the end. The evidence is behavioral. Does your system know which files to read? Can it create or edit files? Can it execute commands and observe their results? Can it preserve state across steps? Can it report a result that another person or program can evaluate? If yes, you are designing agency. If you only ask for a snippet, you are using generation.</p>
          <p>The title does not require full autonomy. TAC-1 demonstrates a tiny serial workflow with a person choosing when to launch it. The role begins as soon as you deliberately package actions into a repeatable system. Later tactics can improve triggers, context, focus, and closed loops; none are prerequisites for recognizing the role here.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--example">
          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — walk the course codebase</p>
          <h3>The repository describes a worker, not a product</h3>
          <p>Open the course source at <code>tac/tac-1</code>. Before any demo runs, there is no <code>main_aic.py</code> and no <code>main_tac.py</code>. The seven committed files are instructional and operational:</p>
          <div class="file-map">
            <div class="file-map__item"><code>README.md</code><p>Holds the phase-one and phase-two prompts plus the three launcher commands.</p></div>
            <div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/settings.json</code><p>Declares the tools the agent may use: three file tools, UV, four Git operations, and WebSearch.</p></div>

