335:          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you celebrate that an agent generated 2,000 lines, even though reviewing and repairing those lines consumed more attention than the old workflow. Or you automate a one-off task because the demo looks impressive.</p>
369:        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #1: Stop Coding</h2>
383:          <figure class="diagram">
386:                <title id="cycle-title">Engineering cycles before and after Stop Coding</title>
417:          <figure class="frame-card">
418:            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0414-tactic-stop-coding.jpg" alt="Tactic number one Stop Coding reveal from the lesson" loading="lazy">
419:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:54</span>The headline is intentionally uncomfortable. Read the object of the verb carefully: stop personally <em>typing</em> the code, not producing or understanding it.</figcaption>
447:          <p>Notice what you still do. You read <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. You understand the prompt. You watch the plan. You inspect generated files. You run independent verification commands. You compare the report to real output. You observe how the workflow fails when a permission is removed. Nothing about that work is passive. The difference is that your keystrokes go into instructions and probes, not into application code.</p>
497:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for Stop Coding">
516:        <h2 class="section-title">Big Three → Core Four</h2>
523:          <p>A Core Four diagnosis asks four different questions. Did the agent see the repository state and instruction file it needed? Was the model capable of the reasoning? Did the prompt state a valid starting branch and commit expectation? Did the tool surface actually allow <code>git commit</code>? In TAC-1 stretch drill S3, removing one permission creates exactly this lesson: prompt quality cannot compensate for a missing tool.</p>
524:          <p>Without a diagnostic vocabulary, every failure looks like “bad AI.” With the Core Four, you can isolate the broken dimension before spending another token.</p>
528:          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the Core Four visual</p>
530:          <figure class="diagram">
532:              <div class="core-four" role="img" aria-label="Core Four: context, model, prompt, and tools, with tools highlighted as the added phase-two dimension">
554:          <figure class="frame-card">
555:            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0845-core-four.jpg" alt="Dan's Core Four diagram with context, tools, model, and prompt" loading="lazy">
556:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">14:05</span>The on-screen Core Four is the lesson’s central abstraction. The highlighted teaching point is reliable long-chain execution, not a claim that tool calling was invented here.</figcaption>
563:          <h3>The Big Three and the Core Four</h3>
566:            <p><strong>Big Three:</strong> context, model, prompt. <strong>Core Four:</strong> context, model, prompt, tools. <strong>Precise delta:</strong> prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tools.</p>
594:          <p>Classify the list. <code>Read</code>, <code>Write</code>, and <code>Edit</code> let the agent inspect and change files. <code>uv run</code> lets it execute the generated programs. Four Git patterns let it checkout, inspect/create branches, add files, and commit. <code>WebSearch</code> is the single non-local tool. There is no <code>git push</code> permission.</p>
597:            <thead><tr><th>Observed failure</th><th>First Core Four hypothesis</th><th>Probe</th></tr></thead>
622:          <h3>Failure: treating the Core Four as a memorization diagram</h3>
624:          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> before retrying, write one sentence per dimension: what context was present, which model property mattered, what the prompt required, and which tool/permission enabled the failed transition. Eliminate dimensions with evidence. The Core Four becomes valuable only when it changes your debugging behavior.</p>
640:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Core Four retrieval questions">
641:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the Big Three and the one added dimension.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt; tools are added to form the Core Four.</p></details></div>
704:          <p>A useful test: if you can describe a prompt’s success only by naming generated files, you may be looking at AI coding. If you can describe ordered state transitions and evidence, you are looking at an agentic workflow.</p>
711:          <div class="frame-grid">
712:            <figure class="frame-card">
714:              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:40</span>The baseline: one prompt creates one file. Useful, but the human still owns the surrounding workflow.</figcaption>
716:            <figure class="frame-card">
718:              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">12:28</span>The prompt becomes an execution plan. The todo list externalizes intermediate state so the chain can be carried rather than merely described.</figcaption>
721:          <figure class="frame-card">
723:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:10</span>The after picture: branch checkout, file creation, both UV runs, Git add and commit, todos completed. This trace—not the print statements—is the phase-two artifact.</figcaption>
781:            <li>Verify branch, generated behavior, execution trace, commit message, and report.</li>
822:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is <code>uv run</code> load-bearing in the comparison?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It turns generated code into observed behavior. The agent does not merely write; it executes and can use the outputs as evidence.</p></details></div>
852:          <figure class="diagram">
879:          <p>After introducing the Core Four, Dan asks why reliable agentic coding became possible at this point. His answer is the combination of three elements: a powerful language model that can reason when needed, the ability to consistently call long chains of tools, and the right agent architecture.</p>
880:          <figure class="frame-card">
882:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">15:03</span>The slide explains the form factor. Carry the checklist forward even when model names and interfaces change.</figcaption>
967:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What is transferable when the UI and model names change?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The tactic, Core Four diagnosis, agentic prompt shape, and three-element evaluation checklist.</p></details></div>
995:          <figure class="diagram">
1031:          <div class="frame-grid">
1032:            <figure class="frame-card">
1034:              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">17:43</span>Three launchers, one prompt. The directory view is the architecture in miniature.</figcaption>
1036:            <figure class="frame-card">
1038:              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">18:25</span>The Python implementation demystifies the pattern: read, invoke, capture, print, propagate status.</figcaption>
1042:          <figure class="frame-card">
1044:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">20:05</span>The report lands back in the host terminal. This closes the first programmable loop: prompt in, engineering actions performed, evidence out.</figcaption>
1213:              <tr><td>Type two tiny programs</td><td>CREATE blocks</td><td>State behavior and review generated implementation</td></tr>
1330:          <p><strong>Failure this exposes:</strong> mistaking “more generated files” for “engineering work carried by an agent.”</p>
1352:            <li>Inspect <code>main_tac.py</code>. It must print <code>hello agentic coding</code> and a concise, single-line definition of AI agents. The exact definition wording is model-generated, so grade the behavior, not a fabricated fixed sentence.</li>
1465:            <li>List real tasks that are AI-coding-prompt-shaped: the needed outcome is primarily generated code.</li>
1486:          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Use when stuck</li><li>Diagnose with Core Four</li></ul>
1519:              <li>□ Big Three and Core Four, with the precise delta.</li>
1537:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What upgrades them to the Core Four?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tools, specifically prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tool calls.</p></details></div>
1538:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does “Stop Coding” forbid, and what does it preserve?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It forbids hand-typing application code as the default instrument. It preserves and intensifies code generation, understanding, review, planning, and closed-loop design.</p></details></div>
