/bin/zsh -lc "sed -n '180,520p' IndyDevDan/book/vol-1-tac/01-hello-agentic-coding.html" in /Users/kvnkishore/WebstormProjects/AgenticEngineer
 succeeded in 0ms:
            <div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/settings.json</code><p>Declares the tools the agent may use: three file tools, UV, four Git operations, and WebSearch.</p></div>
            <div class="file-map__item"><code>programmable/prompt.md</code><p>Packages a self-contained branch → create → run → commit → report workflow.</p></div>
            <div class="file-map__item"><code>programmable/*.{sh,py,ts}</code><p>Calls the same prompt from three host languages, proving the agent is a component.</p></div>
            <div class="file-map__item"><code>.env.sample</code><p>Contains the API-key placeholder used by the headless billing path.</p></div>
            <div class="file-map__item"><code>main_*.py</code><p><strong>Not source files at the start.</strong> They are runtime artifacts created by the agent.</p></div>
          </div>
          <p>The repository’s product is not the two print statements. Its product is a legible mechanism for directing an agent. This is the Agentic Engineer role reduced until no accidental complexity remains.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--try">
          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — 3 minute drill</p>
          <h3>Classify the work by who carries the transitions</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Write down one task where you ask an AI for code, then manually install the answer.</li>
            <li>Underline every transition you still carry: locate, branch, edit, run, inspect, commit, report.</li>
            <li>Rewrite the task as an outcome with explicit actions and a final report.</li>
            <li>Do <em>not</em> run it yet. Circle the actions that would require tools or permissions.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Your first draft is successful if it exposes the workflow you normally perform invisibly. The goal is not an elegant prompt. The goal is to see the human glue.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--fail">
          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
          <h3>Failure: a title change without a delegation change</h3>
          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you say “agentic” because the tool has an agent mode, but you still approve and relay each micro-step. Or you send a long prompt that generates several files but never executes or verifies them.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> ask a falsifiable question: <em>What engineering action can the system complete without me moving information between steps?</em> Start with one closed chain, not a grand autonomy claim. In TAC-1 that chain is branch → write → run → commit → report. Once the system carries it, your role really has moved.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--current">
          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
          <h3>The ceiling is higher; the primitive is unchanged</h3>
          <aside class="current-practice">
            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
            <p>TAC-1 intentionally shows one agent, one terminal, one serial workflow. Current practice can fan the same shape into subagents, background tasks, and worktree-isolated parallel runs. Do not import that complexity into this first lesson. First learn to specify one reliable chain. Parallelism multiplies both good system design and confused system design.</p>
            <p class="current-practice__source">Forward pointer: later TAC lessons scale the seed planted here.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--check">
          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
          <h3>Close the section before revealing answers</h3>
          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for the Agentic Engineer role">
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What behavior, not job title, defines Dan’s Agentic Engineer?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Building systems that can take actions as engineers can—such as branching, writing, running, committing, and reporting.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is an excellent one-shot code prompt still phase-one shaped?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the output is code while the human still carries the engineering workflow around it. Quality does not change who performs the transitions.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is the smallest observable proof that your role has moved?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A system completes at least one meaningful multi-action chain without the human relaying intermediate results, then returns evidence for review.</p></details></div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--teach">
          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
          <div class="teach-back">
            <p><strong>Explain in 60 seconds:</strong> “An Agentic Engineer is not just someone who writes better prompts.” Use one example from the seven-file TAC-1 repository and name the moment when the human stops being workflow glue.</p>
            <p class="fine">Pass if your explanation includes actions, a repeatable system, and a report—not merely “AI does more.”</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-mission">
        <span class="section-number">02</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">The mission: command compute through leverage</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The tactic is emotionally difficult because it asks you to retire a visible skill. The mission supplies the reason: move up the stack from producing keystrokes to designing systems that produce outcomes.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>The faster typist who cannot escape throughput</h3>
          <p>Suppose an engineer doubles their typing speed, memorizes every framework API, and uses autocomplete perfectly. They may produce more code per hour, but every additional unit still consumes their attention. Their throughput is coupled to presence. If they stop, the work stops.</p>
          <p>Now suppose another engineer spends ten minutes creating a reliable workflow that can be fired again. The first run may be slower than hand-typing. The second run costs almost no new design attention. The tenth run makes the original time investment look tiny. This is why Dan says the 10x engineer does not come from 10x time or effort. The multiplier is leverage, and the number could be 2x, 10x, or 100x depending on the leverage point.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — attention versus repeatability</p>
          <h3>Effort is linear; a reusable system bends the curve</h3>
          <figure class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram__canvas">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 820 390" role="img" aria-labelledby="leverage-title leverage-desc">
                <title id="leverage-title">Leverage curve comparing hand work and reusable agentic work</title>
                <desc id="leverage-desc">Hand work rises linearly with every repetition. Agentic work has an initial design investment, then much lower marginal attention for later runs.</desc>
                <defs><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="7" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L0,6 L8,3 z" fill="var(--ink-mute)"/></marker></defs>
                <line x1="90" y1="315" x2="760" y2="315" stroke="var(--ink-mute)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>
                <line x1="90" y1="315" x2="90" y2="45" stroke="var(--ink-mute)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>
                <text x="610" y="355" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">workflow repetitions</text>
                <text x="28" y="210" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)" transform="rotate(-90 28 210)">cumulative human attention</text>
                <path d="M110 295 L700 75" fill="none" stroke="var(--warn)" stroke-width="5"/>
                <text x="540" y="105" fill="var(--warn)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">hand-carry every run</text>
                <path d="M110 295 C145 180 205 155 285 150 C420 142 565 137 700 132" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="6"/>
                <circle cx="190" cy="164" r="8" fill="var(--accent)"/>
                <text x="205" y="190" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">design the workflow once</text>
                <text x="470" y="162" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">low marginal attention</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <figcaption>The first automated run includes design cost. Leverage appears across repetitions, not in a screenshot of run one.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
          <h3>He names the enemy, then changes the identity</h3>
          <p>Dan states the mission with uncomfortable specificity: become an engineer “they” cannot replace—where “they” means your boss, the company, the industry, and AI. The point is not permanent safety; he explicitly says nothing in technology lasts forever. The point is to choose the adaptation with the greatest asymmetric advantage.</p>
          <div class="frame-grid">
            <figure class="frame-card">
              <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0124-mission-irreplaceable.jpg" alt="Mission slide naming the engineer they cannot replace" loading="lazy">
              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">2:04</span>The mission supplies the criterion by which later tactics are judged: do they increase your leverage and make your contribution harder to substitute?</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="frame-card">
              <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0162-commander-of-compute.jpg" alt="Commander of Compute title card from TAC-1" loading="lazy">
              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">2:42</span>The identity shift has a name: commander of compute. Your work moves from producing the artifact to aiming the machinery.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
          <p>The story’s one move is to replace a threatened identity with a higher-order one. If “real engineer” means “person who types the best code,” an agent that types better feels like a rival. If engineering means building systems of leverage that produce valuable outcomes, a capable coding agent becomes new machinery. You are measured by what you can make the machinery reliably accomplish.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
          <h3>Commander of compute</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term">
            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p>
            <p><strong>Commander of compute:</strong> the phase-two engineer who aims powerful models and agentic systems at valuable outcomes, building systems that build systems.</p>
          </aside>
          <p>Plain English: stop treating the model like a faster pair of hands and start treating it like programmable capacity. Commanding does not mean shouting more prompts. It means deciding the target, furnishing context, declaring constraints, granting a bounded tool surface, defining success evidence, and choosing when the workflow should run.</p>
          <p>Dan’s end-state phrase—<strong>self-operating machine</strong>—should be read incrementally. TAC-1 does not claim that a codebase becomes autonomous in one lesson. It shows the smallest seed: one workflow can recreate a branch, files, executions, commit, and report without an interactive human. Every later improvement can move another piece of attention out of the repeated path.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--example">
          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — walk the course codebase</p>
          <h3>Where the leverage lives in <code>programmable/</code></h3>
          <p>Separate the TAC-1 headless example into one-time design and repeatable execution.</p>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>One-time human design</th><th>Repeatable agent execution</th><th>Evidence returned</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Specify a known starting branch</td><td>Checkout <code>demo-agentic-coding</code></td><td>Current branch can be inspected</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Describe both generated files</td><td>Create <code>main_aic.py</code> and <code>main_tac.py</code></td><td>Files exist with reviewable content</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Declare execution commands</td><td>Run both files with <code>uv run</code></td><td>Exact outputs appear in the trace/report</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Declare repository transition</td><td><code>git add .</code> and commit with the specified message</td><td><code>git log</code> exposes the commit</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Require a report</td><td>Return both program outputs</td><td>The launcher prints a human-readable result</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>The two print programs are deliberately disposable. The leverage lives in the durable <code>prompt.md</code> plus launcher pattern. Delete the runtime branch and files, fire the launcher again, and the work reappears. Reproducibility—not code volume—is the first visible multiplier.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--try">
          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — leverage ledger</p>
          <h3>Measure attention, not impressiveness</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Choose one repeated engineering task from your week.</li>
            <li>Write the minutes you spend per repetition and the number of repetitions per month.</li>
            <li>List which steps are judgment and which are deterministic transitions.</li>
            <li>Mark the smallest complete chain an agent could carry while returning evidence to your judgment point.</li>
            <li>Estimate break-even: design minutes ÷ minutes saved per run.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>A boring five-minute workflow repeated 40 times may be a better leverage point than an impressive hour-long task performed once. This chapter does not authorize using any non-course repository in the lab; this ledger is a planning exercise for transfer after mastery.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--fail">
          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
          <h3>Failure: confusing more output with more leverage</h3>
          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you celebrate that an agent generated 2,000 lines, even though reviewing and repairing those lines consumed more attention than the old workflow. Or you automate a one-off task because the demo looks impressive.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> evaluate the ratio Dan’s mission actually cares about: valuable outcome per unit of your attention. Count reruns, intervention points, review load, and evidence quality. A short stable workflow with a clear report is more agentic than a spectacular uncontrolled generation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--current">
          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
          <h3>Period detail changes; leverage does not</h3>
          <aside class="current-practice">
            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
            <p>The course-era Claude Code interface and model names are period detail. Newer interfaces, plan modes, and background execution can reduce friction, but they do not change the leverage test. Dan future-proofs the lesson directly: newer models and a different UI are welcome; do not let them distract from the tactic.</p>
            <p class="current-practice__source">Use today’s tool, but grade it with the same question: how much reliable outcome does one unit of attention purchase?</p>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--check">
          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for leverage and mission">
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Why does Dan say the “10x” number is arbitrary?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the multiplier comes from the leverage point you find. It could be 2x, 10x, or 100x; it does not come from a fixed amount of greater effort.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What makes the programmable demo leverage rather than just automation theater?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The workflow can be recreated repeatedly from a known state with low additional human attention, and it returns evidence through the report.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does a commander of compute decide?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The target, context, constraints, bounded tools, success evidence, and timing of a system that performs engineering work.</p></details></div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--teach">
          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
          <div class="teach-back">
            <p><strong>Explain to a skeptical senior engineer:</strong> why a slower first automated run may still create more leverage than a fast manual run. Use the <code>programmable/prompt.md</code> rerun and distinguish outcome volume from attention saved.</p>
            <p class="fine">Pass if you mention repetition, marginal attention, evidence, and a judgment boundary.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-stop">
        <span class="section-number">03</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #1: Stop Coding</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The provocation is precise: stop <em>hand-typing</em> application code. Keep generating code. Keep understanding code. Move your scarce cycles to planning, reviewing, and building closed loops.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>The ninety-percent trap</h3>
          <p>An agent produces a nearly correct file. One import is wrong and one edge case is missing. You think, “It will be faster if I just fix those two lines.” You open the editor, type the repair, and move on. The task is complete, but the learning loop is broken.</p>
          <p>The immediate edit feels efficient because you compare two minutes of hand work with another agent turn. Dan’s tactic compares something larger: a lifetime of carrying repairs versus learning to design prompts, feedback, and checks that make the system repair itself. Every time you silently take the keyboard back, you hide a failure mode from the system. You get the file, but you do not improve the mechanism that will create the next hundred files.</p>
          <p>This is the behavioral edge of “stop coding.” It does not ask whether you are capable of the fix. It asks whether your personal typing is still the best lever for an outcome you expect to repeat.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — reallocate the engineering cycle</p>
          <h3>Do not remove engineering; move it around the code</h3>
          <figure class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram__canvas">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 860 430" role="img" aria-labelledby="cycle-title cycle-desc">
                <title id="cycle-title">Engineering cycles before and after Stop Coding</title>
                <desc id="cycle-desc">The old cycle centers human attention on typing. The agentic cycle moves human attention to planning, reviewing, and closed-loop design while the agent types and runs.</desc>
                <text x="205" y="42" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">Old allocation</text>
                <circle cx="205" cy="210" r="115" fill="var(--bg-inset)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="2"/>
                <circle cx="205" cy="210" r="62" fill="var(--warn-wash)" stroke="var(--warn)" stroke-width="3"/>
                <text x="205" y="205" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--warn)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="800" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">HUMAN</text>
                <text x="205" y="232" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--warn)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">types code</text>
                <text x="205" y="105" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-base)">plan a little</text>
                <text x="205" y="340" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-base)">review at the end</text>
                <path d="M390 210 L470 210" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="5"/>
                <path d="M458 197 L478 210 L458 223" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="5"/>
                <text x="430" y="185" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-base)">reallocate</text>
                <text x="655" y="42" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">Phase-two allocation</text>
                <circle cx="655" cy="210" r="128" fill="var(--ok-wash)" stroke="var(--ok)" stroke-width="3"/>
                <circle cx="655" cy="210" r="66" fill="var(--accent-wash)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/>
                <text x="655" y="204" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="800" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">AGENT</text>
                <text x="655" y="231" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">types + runs</text>
                <text x="655" y="100" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ok)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">PLAN</text>
                <text x="530" y="222" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ok)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)" transform="rotate(-90 530 222)">REVIEW</text>
                <text x="780" y="220" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ok)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)" transform="rotate(90 780 220)">CLOSE LOOP</text>
                <text x="655" y="345" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ok)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-base)">human judgment surrounds the run</text>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <figcaption>“Stop coding” removes the human from the typing center, not from the engineering system.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
          <h3>The tactic is said twice because the easy interpretation is wrong</h3>
          <p>At 6:47, after establishing the mission and leverage frame, Dan reveals the first tactic: “Stop coding.” He repeats it. Then he sharpens the claim: your hands and your mind are no longer the best tool for the job of writing code. The directness is meant to break a default behavior, not to erase engineering knowledge.</p>
          <figure class="frame-card">
            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0414-tactic-stop-coding.jpg" alt="Tactic number one Stop Coding reveal from the lesson" loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:54</span>The headline is intentionally uncomfortable. Read the object of the verb carefully: stop personally <em>typing</em> the code, not producing or understanding it.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Dan grounds the discomfort in his own history. He spent more than a decade hand-coding across startups and large companies, mastering the familiar craft, and says he misses it. That confession matters pedagogically: the tactic is not offered by someone who never valued coding. It is an argument that the best instrument for code production has changed, and that engineers must move their attention to the work the instrument cannot own.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
          <h3>Stop coding ≠ stop engineering</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term">
            <p class="callout__label">Exact distinction</p>
            <p><strong>Stop coding:</strong> stop typing application code by hand. Continue generating more code than before, understanding what it does, reviewing it, and designing systems that can validate and improve it.</p>
          </aside>
          <p>Three reallocations make the phrase operational:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Planning:</strong> express outcomes, starting state, constraints, ordered actions, and pass conditions before compute begins.</li>
            <li><strong>Reviewing:</strong> inspect behavior, diffs, architecture, risks, and evidence. Code understanding becomes more—not less—important when production accelerates.</li>
            <li><strong>Closed-loop structures:</strong> connect generation to execution and observation so failure becomes feedback the system can act on.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The tactic is therefore a constraint on who makes the keystrokes, not a license to be ignorant. If your agent generates ten times more code, your ability to frame, inspect, and govern code must become more systematic.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--example">
          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — walk the course codebase</p>
          <h3>Zero application-code keystrokes in TAC-1</h3>
          <p>The README’s phase-one prompt contains a description of <code>main_aic.py</code>:</p>
          <pre><code>CREATE main_aic.py:
    print goodbye ai coding</code></pre>
          <p>The course artifact does not ask you to open <code>main_aic.py</code> and type the print statement. You give the instruction to Claude Code. The agent writes it. Later, <code>programmable/prompt.md</code> describes both runtime files and the agent writes both again. The lab’s prime directive extends the same rule to stretch changes: even when you add a branch name to the REPORT or create a fourth launcher, you ask Claude Code to make the edit.</p>
          <p>Notice what you still do. You read <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. You understand the prompt. You watch the plan. You inspect generated files. You run independent verification commands. You compare the report to real output. You observe how the workflow fails when a permission is removed. Nothing about that work is passive. The difference is that your keystrokes go into instructions and probes, not into application code.</p>
          <aside class="callout callout--why">
            <p class="callout__label">Why the constraint matters</p>
            <p>When an exercise is trivial, hand-editing always looks faster. The zero-typing rule makes you practice the control surface before tasks become too large for manual rescue.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--try">
          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — repair without reclaiming the keyboard</p>
          <h3>Use the next imperfection as the exercise</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>During the TAC-1 lab, wait until an output differs from your expectation.</li>
            <li>Before touching the file, state the discrepancy as observed versus expected behavior.</li>
            <li>Give the agent the observation, the expected result, and the command that should prove success.</li>
            <li>Let the agent edit and rerun.</li>
            <li>Review the diff and evidence. If still wrong, repeat with a more precise observation.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>The drill is passed when the system repairs the artifact and you can name what improved in your feedback. Speed is secondary on this first repetition.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--fail">
          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
          <h3>Two opposite misunderstandings</h3>
          <div class="comparison">
            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No more code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> code remains a primary asset that produces features and products. TAC expects you to generate more of it.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> say “stop hand-typing” every time you teach the tactic.</p></div></article>
            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No need to understand code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> faster generation magnifies the cost of weak review and weak system judgment.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> move reading, execution, tests, and diff review into explicit pass criteria.</p></div></article>
          </div>
          <p>A third failure hides between them: ritualistically never touching code while also never reviewing it. That is not Dan’s tactic. The goal is engineering leverage, not ideological purity. The lab’s zero-typing rule is a training constraint that forces better control habits.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--current">
          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
          <h3>The tactic has a public companion</h3>
          <aside class="current-practice">
            <p class="current-practice__label">Current-practice video · plan-approved</p>
            <p>The locked overlay map pairs this lesson’s thesis with Dan’s public treatment, <em>AI Coding is NOT ENOUGH</em>. Use it after you can state the phase distinction yourself; the chapter’s course demo remains the graded evidence.</p>
            <p class="current-practice__source">Metadata should be rechecked at publication; the ID is supplied by the locked extract.</p>
          </aside>
          <div class="video-cards video-cards--single">
            <a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2TIXl2rlA6Q">
              <span class="video-card__type">Operationalizes the tactic</span>
              <strong>AI Coding is NOT ENOUGH</strong>
              <p>Reinforces the phase-one → phase-two thesis and the reason engineering cannot collapse into code generation.</p>
              <span class="video-card__watch">Open video ↗</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--check">
          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for Stop Coding">
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What exactly stops when you follow Tactic #1?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Your hand-typing of application code. Code generation and code understanding continue.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Where do the freed engineering cycles go?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Planning, reviewing, and creating closed-loop structures.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is fixing the last two lines by hand a course failure even when it is faster?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It completes the artifact while hiding the loop’s weakness. You lose the chance to improve agent instructions, feedback, and verification for repeatable future work.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What must increase when agents generate more code?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The quality and systematization of planning, review, validation, and governance.</p></details></div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--teach">
          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
          <div class="teach-back">
            <p><strong>Teach the tactic to a junior developer</strong> without using the phrase “AI is better at coding.” Explain what they stop, what they keep, and what three kinds of work replace typing. Give one example of a good re-prompt after an imperfect file.</p>
            <p class="fine">Pass if the learner could act differently on their next task.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-core-four">
        <span class="section-number">04</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Big Three → Core Four</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The phase change adds one diagnostic dimension: tools. The novelty is not that tools exist. The breakthrough is that a prompt can reliably carry a long chain of tool calls at useful scale and performance.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "sed -n '520,900p' IndyDevDan/book/vol-1-tac/01-hello-agentic-coding.html" in /Users/kvnkishore/WebstormProjects/AgenticEngineer
 succeeded in 0ms:
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>The reroll reflex</h3>
          <p>An agentic run stops before committing. A weak operator submits the same prompt again, perhaps adding “please make sure to commit.” The second run stops too. The operator concludes that the model is flaky.</p>
          <p>A Core Four diagnosis asks four different questions. Did the agent see the repository state and instruction file it needed? Was the model capable of the reasoning? Did the prompt state a valid starting branch and commit expectation? Did the tool surface actually allow <code>git commit</code>? In TAC-1 stretch drill S3, removing one permission creates exactly this lesson: prompt quality cannot compensate for a missing tool.</p>
          <p>Without a diagnostic vocabulary, every failure looks like “bad AI.” With the Core Four, you can isolate the broken dimension before spending another token.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the Core Four visual</p>
          <h3>Four interacting primitives, one newly emphasized dimension</h3>
          <figure class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram__canvas">
              <div class="core-four" role="img" aria-label="Core Four: context, model, prompt, and tools, with tools highlighted as the added phase-two dimension">
                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Context</b><p>What the model can see: repo, files, settings, current state.</p></div>
                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Model</b><p>The reasoning and generation capacity doing the work.</p></div>
                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Prompt</b><p>The outcome, constraints, ordered actions, and report request.</p></div>
                <div class="core-four__cell core-four__cell--tools"><b>Tools</b><p>The bounded actions the agent can execute in a reliable chain.</p></div>
                <span class="core-four__plus">4</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>The Big Three never disappear. Phase two adds tools and makes the interaction among all four operational.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="concept-strip">
            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Context</b><p>“Can it see what matters?”</p></div>
            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Model</b><p>“Can it reason through this?”</p></div>
            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Prompt</b><p>“Does it know the outcome and checks?”</p></div>
            <div class="concept-strip__item concept-strip__item--new"><b>Tools</b><p>“Can it take the required actions?”</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
          <h3>The full trace makes the fourth circle visible</h3>
          <p>After the agentic prompt checks out a branch, writes <code>main_tac.py</code>, runs both programs, stages files, commits, cleans up its todos, and reports, Dan names what changed. AI coding had always involved context, model, and prompt—even if the user did not see all three. Agentic coding adds tools.</p>
          <figure class="frame-card">
            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0845-core-four.jpg" alt="Dan's Core Four diagram with context, tools, model, and prompt" loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">14:05</span>The on-screen Core Four is the lesson’s central abstraction. The highlighted teaching point is reliable long-chain execution, not a claim that tool calling was invented here.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Dan anticipates the obvious objection: tool calling is not new. His answer is exact and portable: “It’s not a question of new. It’s a question of scale and performance.” A single calculator call proves a model can use a tool. A branch → write → run → observe → stage → commit → report chain proves that tool use can form a unit of engineering work.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
          <h3>The Big Three and the Core Four</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term">
            <p class="callout__label">Canonical terms</p>
            <p><strong>Big Three:</strong> context, model, prompt. <strong>Core Four:</strong> context, model, prompt, tools. <strong>Precise delta:</strong> prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tools.</p>
          </aside>
          <p>Tools should not be reduced to a list of commands. The tool dimension has at least three properties:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Availability:</strong> a tool exists that can perform the needed action.</li>
            <li><strong>Permission:</strong> the system allows that action in the current context.</li>
            <li><strong>Chain reliability:</strong> the agent can choose, order, execute, and learn from several calls without losing the goal.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>This explains why all four interact. A correct Git tool with no repository context is useless. A capable model with an ambiguous starting state may choose the wrong branch. A precise prompt asking for a forbidden commit cannot cross the permission boundary. Diagnostics work because you ask which dimension prevented the next state transition.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--example">
          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — <code>.claude/settings.json</code></p>
          <h3>Read the tool surface before running the prompt</h3>
          <p>The lesson’s settings file is the tools circle made concrete:</p>
          <pre><code>{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": [
      "Read", "Write", "Edit",
      "Bash(uv run:*)",
      "Bash(git checkout:*)",
      "Bash(git branch:*)",
      "Bash(git add:*)",
      "Bash(git commit:*)",
      "WebSearch"
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
          <p>Classify the list. <code>Read</code>, <code>Write</code>, and <code>Edit</code> let the agent inspect and change files. <code>uv run</code> lets it execute the generated programs. Four Git patterns let it checkout, inspect/create branches, add files, and commit. <code>WebSearch</code> is the single non-local tool. There is no <code>git push</code> permission.</p>
          <p>Now predict the README workflow. The agent can create the branch because checkout/branch are allowed. It can write <code>main_tac.py</code>. It can run both files with UV. It can add and commit. It cannot push the commit under this allowlist. You have understood the possible behavior before spending tokens.</p>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Observed failure</th><th>First Core Four hypothesis</th><th>Probe</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Agent edits wrong file</td><td>Context or prompt</td><td>Ask which file path it read and where the path was specified.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Agent cannot commit</td><td>Tools/permission</td><td>Inspect <code>.claude/settings.json</code> for <code>Bash(git commit:*)</code>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Agent creates correct code but skips execution</td><td>Prompt or chain planning</td><td>Confirm the RUN block explicitly includes both <code>uv run</code> commands.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Agent reports success with wrong output</td><td>Prompt/report evidence or model reasoning</td><td>Run files independently and require exact output in REPORT.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--try">
          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — permissions audit</p>
          <h3>Checkpoint zero of the lab begins here</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Open <code>tac/tac-1/.claude/settings.json</code>.</li>
            <li>Without running the agent, write the three file tools.</li>
            <li>Write the four allowed Git subcommands and one important absent operation.</li>
            <li>Name the single non-local tool.</li>
            <li>Close the file and recite the answers.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Pass:</strong> Read, Write, Edit; checkout, branch, add, commit; push is not allowed; WebSearch is the non-local tool.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--fail">
          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
          <h3>Failure: treating the Core Four as a memorization diagram</h3>
          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you can chant “context, model, prompt, tools” but respond to every error by rewriting the prompt.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> before retrying, write one sentence per dimension: what context was present, which model property mattered, what the prompt required, and which tool/permission enabled the failed transition. Eliminate dimensions with evidence. The Core Four becomes valuable only when it changes your debugging behavior.</p>
          <p>A related failure is giving the tools circle unlimited power so the workflow cannot be blocked. That avoids permission friction by deleting a safety boundary. TAC-1 teaches the opposite habit: inspect and deliberately bound what the agent may do.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--current">
          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
          <h3>Permissions became richer; the audit habit survived</h3>
          <aside class="current-practice">
            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
            <p>The flat allowlist shown in TAC-1 still communicates the primitive cleanly. Current Claude Code practice also includes permission modes, deny rules, hooks around tool use, sandboxed Bash, and settings layered across user, project, and local scopes. The modern surface is larger; the timeless habit is unchanged: inspect what the agent may do before you run it.</p>
            <p class="current-practice__source">Teaching rule: learn the visible allowlist first, then add richer gates when the workflow requires them.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--check">
          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Core Four retrieval questions">
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the Big Three and the one added dimension.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt; tools are added to form the Core Four.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What exact capability makes the tools dimension phase-two significant?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tool calls.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is Dan’s rebuttal to “tool calling isn’t new”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Novelty is not the deciding issue; the change is useful scale, performance, and reliable long-chain execution.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Which allowed tool proves the agent can commit but not publish?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>Bash(git commit:*)</code> is allowed, while no <code>git push</code> permission appears.</p></details></div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--teach">
          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
          <div class="teach-back">
            <p><strong>Diagnose aloud:</strong> an agent creates the correct files and runs them, but cannot make the requested commit. Walk through all four dimensions, then explain why tools/permission is the leading hypothesis. Finish with Dan’s scale-and-performance answer.</p>
            <p class="fine">Pass if you use evidence rather than “the AI is flaky.”</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-ab">
        <span class="section-number">05</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Two prompts, one monumental difference</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The chapter’s A/B test holds repository, model, and operator constant. Prompt A creates a file. Prompt B carries an engineering workflow. Run both; the delta is the lesson.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>“Both made files, so they are basically the same”</h3>
          <p>A learner runs both prompts and notices that <code>main_aic.py</code> and <code>main_tac.py</code> now exist. They conclude that the second prompt is merely a longer code-generation request. The visible artifacts conceal the real difference because files are the residue of both processes.</p>
          <p>To see the delta, ignore the final directory for a moment and compare state transitions. Before Prompt A, one file is absent; afterward, it exists. Before Prompt B, the repository is on a starting branch with incomplete artifacts; afterward, a named branch exists, a second file exists, two programs have been executed, their output has been observed, changes have been staged and committed with a named message, a todo sequence has drained, and a report has been returned. The second prompt did not merely produce more. It moved the repository through an ordered series of verified states.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — AI coding versus agentic coding</p>
          <h3>Compare verbs, states, and evidence</h3>
          <div class="comparison">
            <article class="comparison__card">
              <div class="comparison__head"><p class="eyebrow">Phase 1</p><h3>AI Coding Prompt</h3><p>A request to create code.</p></div>
              <div class="comparison__body">
                <pre><code>CREATE main_aic.py:
    print goodbye ai coding</code></pre>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Verb:</strong> create</li>
                  <li><strong>State change:</strong> one file appears</li>
                  <li><strong>Execution:</strong> human runs it later</li>
                  <li><strong>Repository action:</strong> none requested</li>
                  <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> file content</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </article>
            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic">
              <div class="comparison__head"><p class="eyebrow">Phase 2</p><h3>Agentic Coding Prompt</h3><p>A runbook for engineering work.</p></div>
              <div class="comparison__body">
                <pre><code>RUN: checkout branch
CREATE: main_tac.py
RUN: execute both files,
     add + commit</code></pre>
                <ul>
                  <li><strong>Verbs:</strong> run, create, run</li>
                  <li><strong>State changes:</strong> branch, file, index, commit</li>
                  <li><strong>Execution:</strong> agent runs both programs</li>
                  <li><strong>Repository action:</strong> explicit Git workflow</li>
                  <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> trace, outputs, commit, report</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
          <p>A useful test: if you can describe a prompt’s success only by naming generated files, you may be looking at AI coding. If you can describe ordered state transitions and evidence, you are looking at an agentic workflow.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
          <h3>Watch the todo list become a repository history</h3>
          <p>Dan first pastes the AI Coding Prompt. Claude Code writes <code>main_aic.py</code>. He calls it familiar: something you have seen a million times. Then he pastes the Agentic Coding Prompt. This time the agent turns the instruction into a checklist and executes the work step by step.</p>
          <div class="frame-grid">
            <figure class="frame-card">
              <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0700-ai-coding-prompt-run.jpg" alt="The AI coding prompt creating main_aic.py in Claude Code" loading="lazy">
              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:40</span>The baseline: one prompt creates one file. Useful, but the human still owns the surrounding workflow.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="frame-card">
              <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0748-agentic-prompt-checklist.jpg" alt="Claude Code turns the agentic prompt into a todo checklist" loading="lazy">
              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">12:28</span>The prompt becomes an execution plan. The todo list externalizes intermediate state so the chain can be carried rather than merely described.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
          <figure class="frame-card">
            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0790-agentic-prompt-results.jpg" alt="Full agentic coding result showing branch, file writing, program output, commit, and completed todos" loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:10</span>The after picture: branch checkout, file creation, both UV runs, Git add and commit, todos completed. This trace—not the print statements—is the phase-two artifact.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Dan’s one move is to make process visible. The agent’s checklist, tool trace, command output, and commit expose a chain that a human would otherwise carry implicitly. That visibility makes the workflow inspectable and, later, programmable.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
          <h3>The Agentic Coding Prompt</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term">
            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p>
            <p><strong>Agentic Coding Prompt:</strong> a prompt containing a sequence of tool calls. In plain English, it is a workflow specification that an agent can execute.</p>
          </aside>
          <p>The README uses a compact runbook grammar: <code>RUN</code>, <code>CREATE</code>, <code>RUN</code>. These headings separate kinds of action. The first RUN establishes repository state. CREATE defines an artifact. The final RUN executes and records the work. In <code>programmable/prompt.md</code>, a fourth heading—<code>REPORT</code>—makes the output contract explicit.</p>
          <p>The headings are not magic syntax. Their value is structural clarity. A weak prompt mixes goal, starting state, implementation, commands, and success into a paragraph. A runbook shape lets both human and model see the sequence. It also makes omission easier to detect. If no REPORT block exists, a headless caller may receive an unhelpful narrative. If no starting branch is declared, reruns may begin from ambiguous state.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--example">
          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — trace every line</p>
          <h3>Read the README prompt as a state machine</h3>
          <pre><code>RUN:
    checkout a new/existing "demo-agentic-coding" git branch

CREATE main_tac.py:
    print "hello agentic coding"
    print a concise explanation of the definition of ai agents

RUN:
    uv run main_aic.py
    uv run main_tac.py
    git add .
    git commit -m "Demo agentic coding capabilities"</code></pre>
          <div class="workflow" aria-label="Agentic prompt state transitions">
            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>1 · Isolate</strong><code>checkout branch</code></div>
            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>2 · Produce</strong><code>create file</code></div>
            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>3 · Observe</strong><code>run both</code></div>
            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>4 · Record</strong><code>add + commit</code></div>
            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>5 · Return</strong><code>trace/report</code></div>
          </div>
          <p>Line one establishes isolation. The CREATE block specifies two observable behaviors without dictating a full implementation. The next two commands turn code into evidence: <code>uv run main_aic.py</code> should print <code>goodbye ai coding</code>; <code>uv run main_tac.py</code> should print <code>hello agentic coding</code> followed by a concise definition. The final two commands preserve the result in Git.</p>
          <p>The five-action delta you should write in Checkpoint 1 is:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Checkout the named branch.</li>
            <li>Create the second file to a behavioral specification.</li>
            <li>Execute both programs and expose their outputs.</li>
            <li>Stage and commit the change with the specified message.</li>
            <li>Manage the sequence and report completion.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>The prompt depends on <code>main_aic.py</code> from the first interactive run. That dependency is why <code>programmable/prompt.md</code> later creates both files: a headless workflow must be self-contained enough to run from its declared starting state.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--try">
          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — reproduce the A/B test</p>
          <h3>Do not substitute reading for running</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>From the cloned TAC-1 repo, start Claude Code.</li>
            <li>Paste the README’s AI Coding Prompt. Confirm <code>main_aic.py</code> exists, then run <code>uv run main_aic.py</code> yourself.</li>
            <li>Before Prompt B, write what did <em>not</em> happen: no branch, no execution by the agent, no commit.</li>
            <li>Paste the Agentic Coding Prompt. Watch the checklist form and drain.</li>
            <li>Verify branch, generated behavior, execution trace, commit message, and report.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="terminal" aria-label="Verification commands">
            <div class="terminal__bar"><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span></div>
            <pre><code>$ git branch --show-current
demo-agentic-coding

$ uv run main_aic.py
goodbye ai coding

$ uv run main_tac.py
hello agentic coding
[one concise AI-agent definition]

$ git log --oneline -1
[hash] Demo agentic coding capabilities</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--fail">
          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
          <h3>Failure: comparing artifacts instead of transitions</h3>
          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> your A/B notes say “Prompt A made one Python file; Prompt B made another Python file.”</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> use verbs and evidence. Write <em>checkout, create, execute, observe, stage, commit, report</em>. Then connect each verb to a tool permission and a verifiable state. The monumental difference is not that Prompt B is longer or that the code is smarter. It is that the prompt carries engineering actions end to end.</p>
          <p><strong>Secondary stall:</strong> the branch already exists, so checkout behavior differs. Return to <code>main</code> and delete <code>demo-agentic-coding</code> between clean reproductions. The prompt says new/existing, so reuse is valid behavior; the clean reset simply makes the demonstration unambiguous.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--current">
          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
          <h3>The runbook shape spread; the lesson still teaches the primitive</h3>
          <aside class="current-practice">
            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
            <p>RUN/CREATE/RUN/REPORT is now recognizable across command files, reusable skills, CI-oriented prompts, and agent workflows. Modern packaging can add schemas and hooks, but do not skip the plain prompt. Its lack of magic is pedagogically useful: every transition is visible, so you can tell what the runtime must provide.</p>
            <p class="current-practice__source">Rule: teach the workflow in raw form before packaging it into a reusable abstraction.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--check">
          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="A/B demo retrieval questions">
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name five actions Prompt B performed that Prompt A did not.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Branch checkout; second specified file; execution of both programs; Git add and commit; todo management and final report.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is <code>uv run</code> load-bearing in the comparison?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It turns generated code into observed behavior. The agent does not merely write; it executes and can use the outputs as evidence.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why does <code>programmable/prompt.md</code> create <code>main_aic.py</code> too?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>To make the headless workflow self-contained rather than dependent on the earlier interactive phase-one run.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What is the most useful definition of an Agentic Coding Prompt here?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A workflow specification containing a sequence of reliably executed tool calls.</p></details></div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--teach">
          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
          <div class="teach-back">
            <p><strong>Draw the comparison from memory.</strong> On the left, write the one state transition of Prompt A. On the right, draw Prompt B as branch → create → run → commit → report. Explain why two final files do not make the processes equivalent.</p>
            <p class="fine">Pass if your listener can point to the exact boundary where code generation becomes engineering workflow execution.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-tool">
        <span class="section-number">06</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Why this tool class works</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The lesson names three essential elements behind reliable agentic coding: a reasoning-capable model, consistent long tool chains, and the right agent architecture. Treat them as a transferable evaluation checklist.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>A model that can code but cannot carry the work</h3>
          <p>You can have a brilliant model that writes the right function in isolation yet loses the goal after six tool calls. You can have a tool-rich shell around a weak model that follows commands literally but cannot recover when repository state differs. You can have both inside an architecture that forgets observations, loops without limits, or exposes the wrong action surface.</p>
          <p>“Which model is best?” is therefore too small a question for agentic coding. The unit under evaluation is the combined system. TAC-1’s agentic prompt requires the system to interpret a runbook, plan it, call file and shell tools in order, preserve outputs as context, and know when the requested end state has been reached. A high benchmark score on code generation does not prove that chain.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — a three-legged platform</p>
          <h3>Remove one element and reliability collapses</h3>
          <figure class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram__canvas">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 840 430" role="img" aria-labelledby="platform-title platform-desc">
                <title id="platform-title">Three essential elements supporting reliable agentic coding</title>
                <desc id="platform-desc">Reasoning model, long tool-chain consistency, and agent architecture support a platform labeled reliable agentic coding at scale.</desc>
                <rect x="120" y="80" width="600" height="86" rx="18" fill="var(--accent-wash)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/>
                <text x="420" y="118" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-family="var(--font-display)" font-weight="700" font-size="var(--text-diagram-xl)">Reliable agentic coding</text>
                <text x="420" y="147" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">at useful scale and performance</text>
                <path d="M170 166 L240 338 L320 338 L335 166 Z" fill="var(--ok-wash)" stroke="var(--ok)" stroke-width="3"/>
                <path d="M335 166 L385 338 L465 338 L505 166 Z" fill="var(--bg-inset)" stroke="var(--ink-soft)" stroke-width="3"/>
                <path d="M505 166 L520 338 L600 338 L670 166 Z" fill="var(--bg-inset)" stroke="var(--ink-soft)" stroke-width="3"/>
                <text x="270" y="226" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ok)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="800" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">MODEL</text>
                <text x="270" y="256" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-sm)">reason when</text><text x="270" y="278" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-sm)">needed</text>
                <text x="420" y="226" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="800" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">CHAINS</text>
                <text x="420" y="256" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-sm)">call tools</text><text x="420" y="278" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-sm)">consistently</text>
                <text x="570" y="226" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-weight="800" font-size="var(--text-diagram-lg)">ARCHITECTURE</text>
                <text x="570" y="256" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-sm)">hold state, route</text><text x="570" y="278" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink-soft)" font-family="var(--font-body)" font-size="var(--text-diagram-sm)">actions, stop</text>
                <rect x="145" y="338" width="550" height="24" rx="12" fill="var(--ink)"/>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <figcaption>A great model is necessary but not sufficient. Long-chain behavior emerges from the combined platform.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
          <h3>From “why now?” to an evaluation frame</h3>
          <p>After introducing the Core Four, Dan asks why reliable agentic coding became possible at this point. His answer is the combination of three elements: a powerful language model that can reason when needed, the ability to consistently call long chains of tools, and the right agent architecture.</p>
          <figure class="frame-card">
            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0903-claude-code-three-elements.jpg" alt="Slide listing Claude Code's three essential elements" loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">15:03</span>The slide explains the form factor. Carry the checklist forward even when model names and interfaces change.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Dan positions Claude Code as the phase-two tool that combined these elements properly first, much as Aider represented phase-one AI coding in his framing. But he immediately protects the tactic from tool lock-in: interfaces will change, models will improve, competitors will copy capabilities. The portable lesson is the checklist, not allegiance to one screen.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
          <h3>Three essential elements</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term">
            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s checklist</p>
            <p><strong>1.</strong> A powerful model that can reason when needed. <strong>2.</strong> Consistent long chains of tool calls. <strong>3.</strong> The right agent architecture.</p>
          </aside>
          <p>In plain English:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The <strong>model</strong> must interpret goals, code, output, and unexpected states rather than pattern-match one response.</li>
            <li>The <strong>chain</strong> must survive many transitions. Reliability compounds: if each step is weak, a long workflow fails often even when individual steps look good.</li>
            <li>The <strong>architecture</strong> must present context and tools, preserve observations, manage turns, expose progress, handle errors, and stop at a useful boundary.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Dan’s historical claim is that reliable agentic coding at scale was not possible before this combination. You do not need to debate the date to use the checklist. Any candidate tool must demonstrate the combined behavior on your workflow.</p>

