 succeeded in 0ms:
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is fixing the last two lines by hand a course failure even when it is faster?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It completes the artifact while hiding the loop’s weakness. You lose the chance to improve agent instructions, feedback, and verification for repeatable future work.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What must increase when agents generate more code?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The quality and systematization of planning, review, validation, and governance.</p></details></div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--teach">
          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
          <div class="teach-back">
            <p><strong>Teach the tactic to a junior developer</strong> without using the phrase “AI is better at coding.” Explain what they stop, what they keep, and what three kinds of work replace typing. Give one example of a good re-prompt after an imperfect file.</p>
            <p class="fine">Pass if the learner could act differently on their next task.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-core-four">
        <span class="section-number">04</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Big Three → Core Four</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The phase change adds one diagnostic dimension: tools. The novelty is not that tools exist. The breakthrough is that a prompt can reliably carry a long chain of tool calls at useful scale and performance.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>The reroll reflex</h3>
          <p>An agentic run stops before committing. A weak operator submits the same prompt again, perhaps adding “please make sure to commit.” The second run stops too. The operator concludes that the model is flaky.</p>
          <p>A Core Four diagnosis asks four different questions. Did the agent see the repository state and instruction file it needed? Was the model capable of the reasoning? Did the prompt state a valid starting branch and commit expectation? Did the tool surface actually allow <code>git commit</code>? In TAC-1 stretch drill S3, removing one permission creates exactly this lesson: prompt quality cannot compensate for a missing tool.</p>
          <p>Without a diagnostic vocabulary, every failure looks like “bad AI.” With the Core Four, you can isolate the broken dimension before spending another token.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the Core Four visual</p>
          <h3>Four interacting primitives, one newly emphasized dimension</h3>
          <figure class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram__canvas">
              <div class="core-four" role="img" aria-label="Core Four: context, model, prompt, and tools, with tools highlighted as the added phase-two dimension">
                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Context</b><p>What the model can see: repo, files, settings, current state.</p></div>
                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Model</b><p>The reasoning and generation capacity doing the work.</p></div>
                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Prompt</b><p>The outcome, constraints, ordered actions, and report request.</p></div>
                <div class="core-four__cell core-four__cell--tools"><b>Tools</b><p>The bounded actions the agent can execute in a reliable chain.</p></div>
                <span class="core-four__plus">4</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>The Big Three never disappear. Phase two adds tools and makes the interaction among all four operational.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="concept-strip">
            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Context</b><p>“Can it see what matters?”</p></div>
            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Model</b><p>“Can it reason through this?”</p></div>
            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Prompt</b><p>“Does it know the outcome and checks?”</p></div>
            <div class="concept-strip__item concept-strip__item--new"><b>Tools</b><p>“Can it take the required actions?”</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--story">
          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
          <h3>The full trace makes the fourth circle visible</h3>
          <p>After the agentic prompt checks out a branch, writes <code>main_tac.py</code>, runs both programs, stages files, commits, cleans up its todos, and reports, Dan names what changed. AI coding had always involved context, model, and prompt—even if the user did not see all three. Agentic coding adds tools.</p>
          <figure class="frame-card">
            <img src="../media/frames/tac-1/t0845-core-four.jpg" alt="Dan's Core Four diagram with context, tools, model, and prompt" loading="lazy">
            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">14:05</span>The on-screen Core Four is the lesson’s central abstraction. The highlighted teaching point is reliable long-chain execution, not a claim that tool calling was invented here.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p>Dan anticipates the obvious objection: tool calling is not new. His answer is exact and portable: “It’s not a question of new. It’s a question of scale and performance.” A single calculator call proves a model can use a tool. A branch → write → run → observe → stage → commit → report chain proves that tool use can form a unit of engineering work.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--name">
          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
          <h3>The Big Three and the Core Four</h3>
          <aside class="callout callout--term">
            <p class="callout__label">Canonical terms</p>
            <p><strong>Big Three:</strong> context, model, prompt. <strong>Core Four:</strong> context, model, prompt, tools. <strong>Precise delta:</strong> prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tools.</p>
          </aside>
          <p>Tools should not be reduced to a list of commands. The tool dimension has at least three properties:</p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Availability:</strong> a tool exists that can perform the needed action.</li>
            <li><strong>Permission:</strong> the system allows that action in the current context.</li>
            <li><strong>Chain reliability:</strong> the agent can choose, order, execute, and learn from several calls without losing the goal.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>This explains why all four interact. A correct Git tool with no repository context is useless. A capable model with an ambiguous starting state may choose the wrong branch. A precise prompt asking for a forbidden commit cannot cross the permission boundary. Diagnostics work because you ask which dimension prevented the next state transition.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--example">
          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — <code>.claude/settings.json</code></p>
          <h3>Read the tool surface before running the prompt</h3>
          <p>The lesson’s settings file is the tools circle made concrete:</p>
          <pre><code>{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": [
      "Read", "Write", "Edit",
      "Bash(uv run:*)",
      "Bash(git checkout:*)",
      "Bash(git branch:*)",
      "Bash(git add:*)",
      "Bash(git commit:*)",
      "WebSearch"
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
          <p>Classify the list. <code>Read</code>, <code>Write</code>, and <code>Edit</code> let the agent inspect and change files. <code>uv run</code> lets it execute the generated programs. Four Git patterns let it checkout, inspect/create branches, add files, and commit. <code>WebSearch</code> is the single non-local tool. There is no <code>git push</code> permission.</p>
          <p>Now predict the README workflow. The agent can create the branch because checkout/branch are allowed. It can write <code>main_tac.py</code>. It can run both files with UV. It can add and commit. It cannot push the commit under this allowlist. You have understood the possible behavior before spending tokens.</p>
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Observed failure</th><th>First Core Four hypothesis</th><th>Probe</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Agent edits wrong file</td><td>Context or prompt</td><td>Ask which file path it read and where the path was specified.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Agent cannot commit</td><td>Tools/permission</td><td>Inspect <code>.claude/settings.json</code> for <code>Bash(git commit:*)</code>.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Agent creates correct code but skips execution</td><td>Prompt or chain planning</td><td>Confirm the RUN block explicitly includes both <code>uv run</code> commands.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Agent reports success with wrong output</td><td>Prompt/report evidence or model reasoning</td><td>Run files independently and require exact output in REPORT.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--try">
          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — permissions audit</p>
          <h3>Checkpoint zero of the lab begins here</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Open <code>tac/tac-1/.claude/settings.json</code>.</li>
            <li>Without running the agent, write the three file tools.</li>
            <li>Write the four allowed Git subcommands and one important absent operation.</li>
            <li>Name the single non-local tool.</li>
            <li>Close the file and recite the answers.</li>
          </ol>
          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Pass:</strong> Read, Write, Edit; checkout, branch, add, commit; push is not allowed; WebSearch is the non-local tool.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--fail">
          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
          <h3>Failure: treating the Core Four as a memorization diagram</h3>
          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you can chant “context, model, prompt, tools” but respond to every error by rewriting the prompt.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> before retrying, write one sentence per dimension: what context was present, which model property mattered, what the prompt required, and which tool/permission enabled the failed transition. Eliminate dimensions with evidence. The Core Four becomes valuable only when it changes your debugging behavior.</p>
          <p>A related failure is giving the tools circle unlimited power so the workflow cannot be blocked. That avoids permission friction by deleting a safety boundary. TAC-1 teaches the opposite habit: inspect and deliberately bound what the agent may do.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--current">
          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
          <h3>Permissions became richer; the audit habit survived</h3>
          <aside class="current-practice">
            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
            <p>The flat allowlist shown in TAC-1 still communicates the primitive cleanly. Current Claude Code practice also includes permission modes, deny rules, hooks around tool use, sandboxed Bash, and settings layered across user, project, and local scopes. The modern surface is larger; the timeless habit is unchanged: inspect what the agent may do before you run it.</p>
            <p class="current-practice__source">Teaching rule: learn the visible allowlist first, then add richer gates when the workflow requires them.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--check">
          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Core Four retrieval questions">
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the Big Three and the one added dimension.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt; tools are added to form the Core Four.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What exact capability makes the tools dimension phase-two significant?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tool calls.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is Dan’s rebuttal to “tool calling isn’t new”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Novelty is not the deciding issue; the change is useful scale, performance, and reliable long-chain execution.</p></details></div>
            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Which allowed tool proves the agent can commit but not publish?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>Bash(git commit:*)</code> is allowed, while no <code>git push</code> permission appears.</p></details></div>
          </section>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--teach">
          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
          <div class="teach-back">
            <p><strong>Diagnose aloud:</strong> an agent creates the correct files and runs them, but cannot make the requested commit. Walk through all four dimensions, then explain why tools/permission is the leading hypothesis. Finish with Dan’s scale-and-performance answer.</p>
            <p class="fine">Pass if you use evidence rather than “the AI is flaky.”</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-ab">
        <span class="section-number">05</span>
        <h2 class="section-title">Two prompts, one monumental difference</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The chapter’s A/B test holds repository, model, and operator constant. Prompt A creates a file. Prompt B carries an engineering workflow. Run both; the delta is the lesson.</p>

        <div class="move move--hook">
          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
          <h3>“Both made files, so they are basically the same”</h3>
          <p>A learner runs both prompts and notices that <code>main_aic.py</code> and <code>main_tac.py</code> now exist. They conclude that the second prompt is merely a longer code-generation request. The visible artifacts conceal the real difference because files are the residue of both processes.</p>
          <p>To see the delta, ignore the final directory for a moment and compare state transitions. Before Prompt A, one file is absent; afterward, it exists. Before Prompt B, the repository is on a starting branch with incomplete artifacts; afterward, a named branch exists, a second file exists, two programs have been executed, their output has been observed, changes have been staged and committed with a named message, a todo sequence has drained, and a report has been returned. The second prompt did not merely produce more. It moved the repository through an ordered series of verified states.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="move move--picture">
          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — AI coding versus agentic coding</p>

