         <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #1: Stop Coding</h2>
         <p class="section-lead">The provocation is precise: stop <em>hand-typing</em> application code. Keep generating code. Keep understanding code. Move your scarce cycles to planning, reviewing, and building closed loops.</p>
 
+        <figure class="generated-plate">
+          <img src="../media/generated/plate-stop-coding.png" alt="Stop Coding teaching plate showing hands leaving the keyboard while an agent coordinates several engineering workflows" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy">
+          <figcaption>“Stop coding” reallocates human attention from typing to directing, reviewing, and improving the workflow.</figcaption>
+        </figure>
+
         <div class="move move--hook">
           <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
           <h3>The ninety-percent trap</h3>
@@ -516,6 +525,11 @@
         <h2 class="section-title">Big Three → Core Four</h2>
         <p class="section-lead">The phase change adds one diagnostic dimension: tools. The novelty is not that tools exist. The breakthrough is that a prompt can reliably carry a long chain of tool calls at useful scale and performance.</p>
 
+        <figure class="generated-plate">
+          <img src="../media/generated/infographic-core-four.png" alt="Core Four teaching plate with four large cards labeled Context, Model, Prompt, and Tools" width="1806" height="871" loading="lazy">
+          <figcaption>Use the Core Four as a diagnostic scan: context, model, prompt, and tools.</figcaption>
+        </figure>
+
         <div class="move move--hook">
           <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
           <h3>The reroll reflex</h3>

