   372	        <span class="section-number">03</span>
   373	        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #1: Stop Coding</h2>
   374	        <p class="section-lead">The provocation is precise: stop <em>hand-typing</em> application code. Keep generating code. Keep understanding code. Move your scarce cycles to planning, reviewing, and building closed loops.</p>
   375	
   376	        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate">
   377	          <img src="../media/generated/plate-stop-coding.png" alt="Stop Coding teaching plate showing hands leaving the keyboard while an agent coordinates several engineering workflows" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy">
   378	          <figcaption>“Stop coding” reallocates human attention from typing to directing, reviewing, and improving the workflow.</figcaption>
   379	        </figure>
   380	
   381	        <div class="move move--hook">
   382	          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
   383	          <h3>The ninety-percent trap</h3>
   384	          <p>An agent produces a nearly correct file. One import is wrong and one edge case is missing. You think, “It will be faster if I just fix those two lines.” You open the editor, type the repair, and move on. The task is complete, but the learning loop is broken.</p>
   385	          <p>The immediate edit feels efficient because you compare two minutes of hand work with another agent turn. Dan’s tactic compares something larger: a lifetime of carrying repairs versus learning to design prompts, feedback, and checks that make the system repair itself. Every time you silently take the keyboard back, you hide a failure mode from the system. You get the file, but you do not improve the mechanism that will create the next hundred files.</p>
   518	            <p class="fine">Pass if the learner could act differently on their next task.</p>
   519	          </div>
   520	        </div>
   521	      </section>
   522	
   523	      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-core-four">
   524	        <span class="section-number">04</span>
   525	        <h2 class="section-title">Big Three → Core Four</h2>
   526	        <p class="section-lead">The phase change adds one diagnostic dimension: tools. The novelty is not that tools exist. The breakthrough is that a prompt can reliably carry a long chain of tool calls at useful scale and performance.</p>
   527	
   528	        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate">
   529	          <img src="../media/generated/infographic-core-four.png" alt="Core Four teaching plate with four large cards labeled Context, Model, Prompt, and Tools" width="1806" height="871" loading="lazy">
   530	          <figcaption>Use the Core Four as a diagnostic scan: context, model, prompt, and tools.</figcaption>
   531	        </figure>
   532	
   533	        <div class="move move--hook">
   534	          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
   535	          <h3>The reroll reflex</h3>
   536	          <p>An agentic run stops before committing. A weak operator submits the same prompt again, perhaps adding “please make sure to commit.” The second run stops too. The operator concludes that the model is flaky.</p>
   537	          <p>A Core Four diagnosis asks four different questions. Did the agent see the repository state and instruction file it needed? Was the model capable of the reasoning? Did the prompt state a valid starting branch and commit expectation? Did the tool surface actually allow <code>git commit</code>? In TAC-1 stretch drill S3, removing one permission creates exactly this lesson: prompt quality cannot compensate for a missing tool.</p>
   538	          <p>Without a diagnostic vocabulary, every failure looks like “bad AI.” With the Core Four, you can isolate the broken dimension before spending another token.</p>
